{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791057447,"nonce":"9NmRRqgoEchePrWF05n+LakV5v8=","title":"Horizontal subpixel glyph positioning, so letter spacing stays even at small sizes","message":"Glyph bounds came back on whole device pixels, so every gap between\nletters could be off by up to half a pixel. At small sizes that reads as\nuneven letter spacing.\n\nA run may now opt into rasterizing each glyph at one of a few horizontal\nsubpixel offsets and placing it on a whole pixel, which keeps the\nspacing even without resampling the raster.\n\nAPI: TextRenderer::prepare_text_subpixel takes\nOption\u003cSubpixelX { phases: NonZeroU8, origin_x: f32 }\u003e. None is the old\nbehaviour, so rollback is passing None and the default path is\nuntouched. The two values travel as one type because they are only\nmeaningful together, and NonZeroU8 makes phases == 0 unrepresentable\nrather than a divide by zero in the phase arithmetic.\n\nDeliberately NOT on LayoutOptions, which is what ashui originally\nproposed: LayoutOptions keys the shaping cache, so a position-dependent\nfield would make every run at a new x miss the cache and re-shape\nthrough HarfBuzz. The phase split happens after layout, where positioned\nglyphs become GPU instances, so shaping stays position-independent.\n\nBlinc's own callers do not pass Some yet; the entry point exists for\ncallers that know nothing transforms the run.\n\n\nGlyphKey gains the phase, so a glyph cached at N phases occupies N atlas\nentries. tests/subpixel_cost.rs measures it rather than guessing:\nthe same text over a spread of origins went from 16 atlas entries at\nwhole-pixel to 45 at three phases, 2.81x, against a theoretical ceiling\nof 3x. The test asserts the 3x bound so a regression in the sharing\ncannot pass unnoticed.\n\n\nTwo bounds, both tested:\n\n- A pressure limit. Above 75% atlas utilization no NEW phased raster is\n  created; phase 0 is used instead. Checked after the cache lookup, so a\n  glyph that already has its phases keeps even spacing as the atlas\n  fills.\n- A hard fallback. If a phased insert needs growth the atlas cannot do,\n  it retries at phase 0 rather than returning AtlasFull. A phase is\n  cosmetic, so the right failure is uneven spacing, not a dropped glyph.\n\nSubpixelStats counts phased rasters and fallbacks, so the cost is\nobservable at runtime and not just in a test.\n\nRequested by the ashui session; tracked there as 2a1fc12.","files":null}]}